    },
    {
      "id": "oROY0hApgIr6q7ndJ1q8M",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -13375.129806326331,
      "y": 12446.636962780041,
      "width": 632,
      "height": 1845,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "lTkZ7cLrDfGbg0MGGgAIY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1437131646,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 608667932,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784655845153,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- K8s resource requests/limits: requests guarantee scheduling headroom, limits cap noisy-neighbor blast radius\n  - under-set requests silently throttle CPU (no OOM, just p99 latency creep) — always load-test before setting\n- Liveness vs readiness vs startup probes are different contracts\n  - liveness restarts a wedged process; readiness pulls a pod from the Service endpoint list without restarting it; startup probe protects slow-booting apps from a premature liveness kill\n- Pod Disruption Budgets (PDBs) protect availability during voluntary disruptions (node drains, cluster upgrades)\n  - set minAvailable/maxUnavailable per-Deployment, not cluster-wide, or upgrades can stall indefinitely\n- Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) should scale on the metric that actually predicts load (custom/external metrics via Prometheus adapter), not just CPU — CPU-only HPA lags request-queue-driven bursts\n- Secrets management: never plaintext env vars or ConfigMaps for credentials\n  - use External Secrets Operator / Vault / AWS Secrets Manager + KMS, with rotation and least-privilege IRSA/Workload Identity bindings, not static keys baked into images\n- CI/CD deployment strategy tradeoffs (the judgment interviewers actually probe):\n  - rolling: default, cheap, but a bad version is live for real users during rollout — needs fast, automated health-gated rollback\n  - blue-green: instant cutover + instant rollback, doubles infra cost during the swap, and stateful/DB migrations must be backward-compatible across both colors\n  - canary: lowest blast radius, catches regressions on a slice of real traffic first, but needs traffic-shaping (mesh/ingress) + automated metric-based promotion, not a human eyeballing a dashboard\n- Terraform state is the single source of truth for infra — treat it like production data\n  - remote backend (S3+DynamoDB lock, GCS, Terraform Cloud) is non-negotiable; local state + no locking = concurrent-apply corruption\n- Immutable infrastructure: never patch a running instance/container — rebuild the image/AMI and redeploy\n  - eliminates configuration drift and makes rollback trivial (redeploy the prior artifact instead of reverse-patching)\n- Drift detection: run `terraform plan` (or a drift-detection job) on a schedule against live state, not just at deploy time\n  - manual console/CLI changes are the #1 source of \"works in staging, prod is different\" incidents\n- Golden signals for any service: latency, traffic, errors, saturation (Google SRE book)\n  - dashboards should default to these four before anything custom — they answer \"is this on fire\" in one glance\n- SLOs + error budgets turn reliability into a product decision, not a vibe\n  - a healthy error budget authorizes shipping risky changes; a burned budget should auto-freeze non-critical deploys\n- Distributed tracing (OpenTelemetry + Jaeger/Tempo/X-Ray) is what makes microservice latency debuggable\n  - correlation/trace IDs must propagate through every hop, including async queues, or the trace just dead-ends\n- On-call/incident response: runbooks + clear severity levels + a named incident commander during any SEV1\n  - blameless postmortems focused on systemic/process fixes, not blaming the engineer who pushed the change\n- Cost optimization (FinOps) is an engineering practice, not just a finance report\n  - right-size before autoscaling masks waste, use spot/preemptible for stateless/batch workloads, and tag every resource by team/service so cost is attributable, not just aggregate",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- K8s resource requests/limits: requests guarantee scheduling headroom, limits cap noisy-neighbor blast radius\n  - under-set requests silently throttle CPU (no OOM, just p99 latency creep) — always load-test before setting\n- Liveness vs readiness vs startup probes are different contracts\n  - liveness restarts a wedged process; readiness pulls a pod from the Service endpoint list without restarting it; startup probe protects slow-booting apps from a premature liveness kill\n- Pod Disruption Budgets (PDBs) protect availability during voluntary disruptions (node drains, cluster upgrades)\n  - set minAvailable/maxUnavailable per-Deployment, not cluster-wide, or upgrades can stall indefinitely\n- Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) should scale on the metric that actually predicts load (custom/external metrics via Prometheus adapter), not just CPU — CPU-only HPA lags request-queue-driven bursts\n- Secrets management: never plaintext env vars or ConfigMaps for credentials\n  - use External Secrets Operator / Vault / AWS Secrets Manager + KMS, with rotation and least-privilege IRSA/Workload Identity bindings, not static keys baked into images\n- CI/CD deployment strategy tradeoffs (the judgment interviewers actually probe):\n  - rolling: default, cheap, but a bad version is live for real users during rollout — needs fast, automated health-gated rollback\n  - blue-green: instant cutover + instant rollback, doubles infra cost during the swap, and stateful/DB migrations must be backward-compatible across both colors\n  - canary: lowest blast radius, catches regressions on a slice of real traffic first, but needs traffic-shaping (mesh/ingress) + automated metric-based promotion, not a human eyeballing a dashboard\n- Terraform state is the single source of truth for infra — treat it like production data\n  - remote backend (S3+DynamoDB lock, GCS, Terraform Cloud) is non-negotiable; local state + no locking = concurrent-apply corruption\n- Immutable infrastructure: never patch a running instance/container — rebuild the image/AMI and redeploy\n  - eliminates configuration drift and makes rollback trivial (redeploy the prior artifact instead of reverse-patching)\n- Drift detection: run `terraform plan` (or a drift-detection job) on a schedule against live state, not just at deploy time\n  - manual console/CLI changes are the #1 source of \"works in staging, prod is different\" incidents\n- Golden signals for any service: latency, traffic, errors, saturation (Google SRE book)\n  - dashboards should default to these four before anything custom — they answer \"is this on fire\" in one glance\n- SLOs + error budgets turn reliability into a product decision, not a vibe\n  - a healthy error budget authorizes shipping risky changes; a burned budget should auto-freeze non-critical deploys\n- Distributed tracing (OpenTelemetry + Jaeger/Tempo/X-Ray) is what makes microservice latency debuggable\n  - correlation/trace IDs must propagate through every hop, including async queues, or the trace just dead-ends\n- On-call/incident response: runbooks + clear severity levels + a named incident commander during any SEV1\n  - blameless postmortems focused on systemic/process fixes, not blaming the engineer who pushed the change\n- Cost optimization (FinOps) is an engineering practice, not just a finance report\n  - right-size before autoscaling masks waste, use spot/preemptible for stateless/batch workloads, and tag every resource by team/service so cost is attributable, not just aggregate",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
