    {
      "id": "zqaXcnVNZ01TUn6zBvEqe",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -13375.129806326331,
      "y": 14439.636962780041,
      "width": 632,
      "height": 645,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "9Vt8nL5XiFIIV0-yBC9qN",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 388542597,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 335991803,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784655845153,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Google SRE Book — \"Site Reliability Engineering\" (sre.google/books) — golden signals, SLOs/error budgets, incident response, canonical for the ops/on-call lens\n- Kubernetes official docs (kubernetes.io/docs) — probes, PDBs, HPA, resource management are all documented with the exact field semantics interviewers expect\n- AWS Well-Architected Framework (aws.amazon.com/architecture/well-architected) — the Reliability and Cost Optimization pillars map directly onto FinOps and resilience judgment\n- Google Cloud Architecture Framework (cloud.google.com/architecture/framework) — GCP's equivalent to Well-Architected; reliability + operational excellence pillars\n- Azure Well-Architected Framework (learn.microsoft.com/azure/well-architected) — same pillars, useful for cross-cloud comparison in interviews\n- HashiCorp Terraform docs (developer.hashicorp.com/terraform) — remote state, locking, and drift are covered directly in the official \"State\" section\n- roadmap.sh/devops — free, community-maintained DevOps/SRE learning roadmap, good for gap-checking before an interview\n- \"The Phoenix Project\" / \"The DevOps Handbook\" (Kim, Humble, Debois, Willis) — the canonical narrative + practice reference for CI/CD culture and deployment risk",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Google SRE Book — \"Site Reliability Engineering\" (sre.google/books) — golden signals, SLOs/error budgets, incident response, canonical for the ops/on-call lens\n- Kubernetes official docs (kubernetes.io/docs) — probes, PDBs, HPA, resource management are all documented with the exact field semantics interviewers expect\n- AWS Well-Architected Framework (aws.amazon.com/architecture/well-architected) — the Reliability and Cost Optimization pillars map directly onto FinOps and resilience judgment\n- Google Cloud Architecture Framework (cloud.google.com/architecture/framework) — GCP's equivalent to Well-Architected; reliability + operational excellence pillars\n- Azure Well-Architected Framework (learn.microsoft.com/azure/well-architected) — same pillars, useful for cross-cloud comparison in interviews\n- HashiCorp Terraform docs (developer.hashicorp.com/terraform) — remote state, locking, and drift are covered directly in the official \"State\" section\n- roadmap.sh/devops — free, community-maintained DevOps/SRE learning roadmap, good for gap-checking before an interview\n- \"The Phoenix Project\" / \"The DevOps Handbook\" (Kim, Humble, Debois, Willis) — the canonical narrative + practice reference for CI/CD culture and deployment risk",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
